Interviewee A

How do you think of graffiti in public spaces?
I see graffiti as a form of public expression. When it’s done thoughtfully, it can transform ordinary walls into cultural canvases that reflect community identity.

If you were to collaborate with others, would you do so?
Absolutely. Collaboration allows different styles and perspectives to come together. It makes the artwork richer and more meaningful.

In a graffiti creation, what kind of role do you prefer to play?
I enjoy brainstorming themes and sketching designs, but I’m also comfortable filling in colors and details. I like being flexible depending on the group’s needs.

What is your impression of street graffiti? What positive or negative experiences has it brought to you?
Street graffiti feels bold and alive. The positive side is the creativity and energy it brings to neglected areas. The negative is sometimes it’s seen as vandalism, which can overshadow the artistic side.

If there was a large interactive projection screen in the square, allowing people to participate remotely, would you be interested?
Yes, that sounds amazing. It would open up participation to more people, especially those who can’t be there physically. It would feel like a true community artwork.

Interviewee B

How do you think of graffiti in public spaces?
I think it’s a powerful way to claim space and share ideas, but it depends on intention. If it’s just tagging, I’m less interested, but if it’s art, it adds value.

If you were to collaborate with others, would you do so?
I usually prefer working alone, but I’m open to collaboration if the project has a strong vision and clear roles.

In a graffiti creation, what kind of role do you prefer to play?
I prefer being the one who outlines the main shapes and sets the tone for the piece. I like leading the creative direction.

What is your impression of street graffiti? What positive or negative experiences has it brought to you?
For me, graffiti has been inspiring—I’ve discovered artists I admire just by walking around the city. The negative side is when it’s dismissed as “messy” by people who don’t understand the art.

If there was a large interactive projection screen in the square, allowing people to participate remotely, would you be interested?
Yes, I’d try it. It’s an innovative way to merge digital and street art. It might feel different from spray paint, but it could be fun to explore.
